s is a blunder.\n3.  **P: c4-c3**: Pushing the pawn further attacks b2 and d2, but c3 is defended by the White Queen on c2 (and pawns). Pushing here would lose the pawn for insufficient compensation.\n4.  **Q: d8-d4**: Centralizes the Queen but exposes it to attack by Ng1-f3 in the subsequent turns, leading to loss of tempo.\n5.  **P: e7-e5**: A strong alternative to claim the center, but N: g8-f6 provides more flexibility and safety for the King.\n\nReason: N: g8-f6 is the most principled move. It prioritizes King safety and efficient development. It avoids the tactical pitfalls of moves like B:c8-f5 and consolidates Black's position to support the advanced c4 pawn later.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6"}
