 key squares. However, it leaves c4 to be captured (`N: a3xc4`), allowing White to alleviate the pressure.\n3.  **`P: b7-b5`**: This attempts to defend c4 with a pawn chain but fails tactically because White can play `N: a3xb5` (forking/attacking), winning a pawn and destroying Black\'s structure.\n4.  **`B: c8-f5`**: Develops with tempo against the Queen, but sacrifices the c4 pawn.\n\nReason: `B: c8-e6` is the most strategically sound move as it preserves the material advantage and spatial control (the "cramp" on White) without incurring tactical deficits. It harmoniously combines development with defense.\n\nAnswer: B: c8-e6'}
